# Build context allow-list.
#
# Deny everything, then name what a build actually reads. The alternative — a
# deny-list — is the one that rots: `screenshots/`, `openspec/`, `tests/` and
# `deploy/` were all in the context of every build of this repo — 17.33 MB
# across 776 files, against the 3.21 MB / 120 files a build actually reads —
# and every new directory would have joined them by default. An allow-list
# makes a forgotten entry a loud build failure ("file not found") instead of a
# silently fatter context and a slower build.
#
# Two Dockerfiles read this context and both are covered:
#   Dockerfile              — requirements.txt, alembic{.ini,/}, src/, scripts/,
#                             docker/entrypoint.sh
#   docker/test.Dockerfile  — requirements.txt, requirements-dev.txt (the suite
#                             itself is bind-mounted at run time, not copied)
#
# Note that `.git/` is excluded here, so nothing in the image can carry the
# repository's history. That matters more than the size: this is a public fork
# of a private-vault server, and the image is published to GHCR.

*

!alembic/
!alembic.ini
!docker/entrypoint.sh
!requirements-dev.txt
!requirements.txt
!scripts/
!src/

# Re-excluded after the allow-list, because these live *inside* the directories
# above. Host-built bytecode is for the host's interpreter and platform; the
# image compiles its own (see the Dockerfile's compileall).
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
